**PRD: The Family Pantry – Marketing Website MVP**

**Version:** 1.0  
**Date:** August 1, 2026  
**Status:** Ready for implementation  
**Audience:** Coding agent (frontend focus)

---

### 1. Purpose & Goals

Build a simple, warm, invitation-focused marketing website for **The Family Pantry** (premium family food brand, Est. 2026).

**Core “Why” (must drive all messaging):**  
People don’t buy what you do, they buy why you do it.  
The Family Pantry exists so families can **Gather** together, **Prepare** something delicious, and **Share** it with those they love.  
The products help people reach the vision of a table set with an amazing spread.

**Site feel:** An open invitation to join the family — not a pure shopping cart.  
**Primary goals of this MVP:**
- Kick off the marketing campaign and start learning
- Establish credibility with friends/family (consumers) and potential partners/suppliers
- Capture interest via “Join the Family”

**Success look:** Visitors feel welcomed, understand the why, see the emotional payoff (family at the table), and take the invitation to join.

---

### 2. Scope

**In scope (MVP):**
- Home / Landing page
- Products listing page (4 product cards)
- 4 individual Product Detail pages
- About Us page
- Contact page
- Global header + footer
- Prominent “Join the Family” CTA on every page (routes to Contact or Products as contextually appropriate)
- Placeholder “Add to Cart” on product detail pages
- Responsive design, ADA compliant, SEO-aware
- Easy theming (CSS variables for colors/branding)

**Out of scope:**
- Real e-commerce / cart / checkout / payments
- User accounts / login
- Backend logic, database, or API implementation details (FastAPI + Postgres will come later)
- Blog, secondary pages, or advanced features

**Tech notes (frontend):**
- React (modern best practices)
- Fully responsive (mobile-first)
- ADA compliant (semantic HTML, proper contrast, keyboard navigation, alt text, ARIA where needed)
- Templated / easy to re-brand (use CSS custom properties for colors, fonts, etc.)
- Monolith repo structure is fine
- Backend (Python/FastAPI) is deferred — frontend can use static/placeholder data for now

---

### 3. Brand & Visual Direction

- **Logo:** Use the provided circular logo (jars + herbs, “Established 2026”, “THE FAMILY PANTRY”, “GATHER • PREPARE • SHARE”).
- **Colors:** Warm, inviting, fairly neutral palette. Implement via CSS variables so it can evolve easily.
- **Overall aesthetic:** Warm, approachable, premium-but-homey. Think natural materials, soft lighting, real family moments.
- **Product visuals:** Square glass bottles with natural wood lids. Labeling matches the logo/branding style.
- **Lifestyle imagery:** Families gathered around a dinner table enjoying an amazing spread (multi-generational, warm, joyful, real food). Use high-quality placeholders (Unsplash-style or generated) for now.
- **Tone of voice:** Warm, inclusive, inviting, never salesy. Always point back to the shared table experience.

---

### 4. Global Elements

**Header:**
- Logo (links to Home)
- Navigation: Home, Products, About, Contact
- Primary CTA button: “Join the Family” (contextually links to Contact or Products)

**Footer (standard on every page):**
- Logo or wordmark
- Navigation links
- Social media links (placeholders)
- Address, phone, email (placeholders)
- Copyright “© 2026 The Family Pantry”
- Keep simple and clean

**SEO:**
- Unique `<title>` and meta description per page
- Semantic headings
- Descriptive alt text on all images
- Clean URL structure

**Accessibility:** ADA compliant (WCAG 2.1 AA target).

---

### 5. Page Specifications

#### 5.1 Home / Landing
- **Hero:** Large warm image of family at the dinner table with an amazing spread. Overlay or paired with logo + tagline “Gather • Prepare • Share” + short headline that sells the vision + primary CTA “Join the Family”.
- Short “Why we exist” section (the credo).
- Teaser of the four products (small cards or strip that links to Products page).
- Invitation language that speaks to both friends/family and partners/suppliers.
- Final CTA section.

#### 5.2 Products (Listing)
- Page title + short intro that ties products back to the shared-table vision.
- Clean responsive grid of **4 product cards**.
- Each card:
  - Product image (square glass bottle, wood lid, matching label)
  - Product name
  - One-sentence benefit focused on the experience
  - “Join the Family” or “View Product” link
- Page-level CTA.

**Products:**
1. Family Taco Night
2. Back Porch (Homestyle) Ranch
3. Backyard Steak
4. Kitchen Essential

#### 5.3 Individual Product Detail Pages (×4)
- Product image (square glass bottle with wood lid)
- Supporting lifestyle image: family at the table using/enjoying that specific product
- Product name
- Short description (placeholder) focused on how it helps create the shared moment
- Price (placeholder, e.g. “$12.99”)
- Placeholder “Add to Cart” button (non-functional for MVP — can show a simple “Coming soon – Join the Family to be notified” message or link to Contact)
- Secondary CTA “Join the Family”

#### 5.4 About Us
- Short intro paragraph focused on the **why** (Gather • Prepare • Share and the vision of the table).
- Longer placeholder section about “the families involved” (can be filled with real stories later).
- Note about Est. 2026 and building alongside partners and suppliers.
- Warm invitation + CTA “Join the Family”.

#### 5.5 Contact
- Warm intro text that frames contacting as joining the family / starting the conversation.
- Simple form:
  - Name
  - Email
  - Optional dropdown or radio: “I’m interested in buying / partnering / supplying / just saying hello”
  - Message textarea
  - Submit button (“Join the Family” or “Send Message”)
- Form is frontend-only for now (can console.log or show success message).
- Contact details repeated from footer.

---

### 6. CTA Behavior Summary

- Primary button text everywhere: **“Join the Family”**
- Routing is contextual and appropriate:
  - Header / general sections → Contact page
  - Product listing or product-focused sections → Products page or specific product page
- Goal is always invitation and interest capture, not a hard sell.

---

### 7. Content Placeholders

All copy should be warm placeholder text that can be easily replaced.  
Focus every piece of content on the emotional destination (the set table and shared moment) rather than product features alone.

---

### 8. Implementation Notes for Coding Agent

- Start with a clean React project structure.
- Use CSS variables for all brand colors, fonts, spacing so re-theming is trivial.
- Make components reusable (Header, Footer, ProductCard, CTA button, etc.).
- Images: use placeholder services or local placeholder assets with clear naming.
- Ensure excellent mobile experience and keyboard/screen-reader accessibility.
- Keep the codebase simple and well-commented for future backend integration.

---

